Frontend, Backend, and Full-Stack Development\par\par
The differences lie in the area of a website or application they address:\par\par
\b Frontend Development (Client-Side):\b0  Deals with the user interface (UI) and user experience (UX) — everything the user sees and interacts with directly in their web browser.\par
	ab \b Focus:\b0  Layout, design, interactivity, and performance on the client's machine.\par
	ab \b Languages/Technologies:\b0  HTML (structure), CSS (style), and JavaScript (interactivity).\par
	ab \b Real-world Example:\b0  The visual layout of an Amazon product page, including the images, buttons (like "Add to Cart"), and the way a drop-down menu works.\par\par
\b Backend Development (Server-Side):\b0  Deals with the server, application logic, and database. It's the "behind-the-scenes" technology that handles data storage, security, and processing requests from the frontend.\par
	ab \b Focus:\b0  Business logic, data storage, security, and communication with the frontend.\par
	ab \b Languages/Technologies:\b0  Python (Django, Flask), Node.js (Express), PHP (Laravel), Java (Spring), etc., and databases like MySQL, PostgreSQL, MongoDB.\par
	ab \b Real-world Example:\b0  When you click "Place Order" on Amazon, the backend processes the payment, checks inventory, updates the database, and sends an order confirmation to your email.\par\par
\b Full-Stack Development:\b0  Involves working with both the frontend and the backend. A full-stack developer is proficient in all layers of the application, from the user interface to the database and server logic.\par
	ab \b Focus:\b0  Building and maintaining a complete, functional web application end-to-end.\par
	ab \b Real-world Example:\b0  A developer who can build the interactive user profile page (frontend), write the server code to handle profile updates (backend), and design the database table to store the user's information (database).\par\par
----------------------------------------\par\par
\b 2. Simple Client-Server Model Diagram\b0\par
The client-server model is a distributed application architecture that partitions tasks between service providers (servers) and service requesters (clients).\par\par
Diagram: \par\par
