----------------------------------------\par\par
\b 3. How a Browser Requests and Displays a Web Page\b0\par
The process is a sequence of steps involving the browser, DNS, and the web server:\par
1. User Input: The user types a URL (e.g., https://www.google.com) into the browser's address bar and presses Enter.\par
2. DNS Lookup: The browser checks its cache to find the IP address corresponding to the domain name. If not found, it queries a DNS server to get the server's IP address.\par
3. Establish Connection (TCP/IP/HTTP): The browser uses the IP address to initiate a connection (usually TCP/IP) with the web server. It then sends an HTTP/HTTPS request (e.g., a GET request for the main page) to the server.\par
4. Server Processing: The web server receives the request, processes it (it might fetch data from a database if it's a dynamic site), and generates an appropriate HTTP response.\par
5. Response Sent: The server sends the response back to the browser. This response includes the HTTP status code (e.g., 200 OK) and the requested data (HTML, CSS, JavaScript files, images, etc.).\par
6. Rendering: The browser begins to render the page:\par
	ab - It first processes the HTML to build the DOM (Document Object Model), which defines the page structure.\par
	ab - It fetches and processes the CSS to build the CSSOM (CSS Object Model), which defines the style.\par
	ab - It combines the DOM and CSSOM to create the render tree and calculates the layout.\par
	ab - The page is painted onto the screen, and JavaScript is executed to add interactivity and final content.\par\par
----------------------------------------\par\par
\b 4. Tools for a Web Development Environment\b0\par
A standard web development environment requires the following essential tools:\par\par
Code Editor (e.g., VS Code)\tab - A text editor designed specifically for writing and editing code. It provides features like syntax highlighting, code completion (IntelliSense), debugging, and integrated terminal.\par
Web Browser (e.g., Chrome, Firefox)\tab - Used to test and display the application. Includes Developer Tools for debugging, inspecting elements, monitoring network activity, and profiling performance.\par
Command Line Interface (CLI)\tab - A text-based interface used to run build scripts, install packages (npm/yarn), run local servers, and manage Git.\par
Version Control System (Git)\tab - Tracks changes to source code, enables collaboration, and manages branches. Hosting services include GitHub and GitLab.\par
Local Web Server (e.g., Live Server, Node.js)\tab - A small server run locally for testing dynamic functionality and AJAX requests.\par
----------------------------------------\par\par
\b 5. What a Web Server Is and Examples\b0\par
A web server stores web content and delivers it to clients upon request using HTTP.\par
Key Functions:\par
1. Accepts HTTP requests from clients.\par
2. Processes the request (fetches a file or executes a script).\par
3. Sends an HTTP response back to the client with the requested resource.\par
Examples of Commonly Used Web Servers:\par
1. Apache HTTP Server\par
2. Nginx\par
3. Microsoft IIS\par
4. Node.js (via Express or similar frameworks)\par
----------------------------------------\par\par
\b 6. Roles in a Web Project\b0\par
Frontend Developer:\par
	ab Focus: Implementing the visual and interactive elements of a website.\par
	ab Responsibilities: Translating UI/UX designs into HTML/CSS/JS, ensuring responsive design, optimizing client-side performance.\par
Backend Developer:\par
	ab Focus: Building and maintaining server-side logic, data access, and APIs.\par
	ab Responsibilities: Writing application logic, handling authentication, managing server-side security, integrating with databases.\par
Database Administrator (DBA):\par
	ab Focus: Designing, implementing, and maintaining databases.\par
	ab Responsibilities: Ensuring data security and integrity, performance tuning, backups and recovery.\par
----------------------------------------\par\par
